---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const updated = 'November 2, 2026';
const repo = 'https://github.com/VisiOffice/VisiPass';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — VisiPass"
  description="VisiPass runs on your computer. It has no accounts, no servers and no analytics, and the browser extension talks only to VisiPass on your machine."
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="pt-28 pb-20">
    <article class="container-narrow max-w-3xl privacy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy policy</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white">Your secrets stay on your computer.</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink-500 text-sm">Last updated {updated}</p>

      <div class="card mt-10">
        <p class="text-ink-200 leading-relaxed">
          VisiPass is software you run on your own computer. There is no VisiPass account and no VisiPass server.
          VisiOffice does not collect, receive, sell or share your passwords, browsing, or anything else you keep in
          VisiPass, and there are no analytics or tracking in the app, the browser extension or this website.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The browser extension</h2>
      <p>
        The extension talks to one thing: the <code>vp</code> program on your computer, through Chrome's native
        messaging. It makes no requests to the internet. To do its job it handles:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The address of the page you are on</strong>, to find the logins saved for that site and to fill only
          that site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The login fields on the page</strong>, when you choose a login to fill or a strong password to use.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A password you submit</strong>, held in the browser's session memory (cleared when the browser
          closes) until you save or dismiss the offer to save it. Pages never get it back.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A screenshot of the visible tab</strong>, only when you click to add a two-factor code from a QR code.
          VisiPass on your computer reads the code from it; the image is not kept.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Three settings</strong> stored in the browser: whether the menu stays quiet, the sites you told it
          never to offer saving on, and which vault new logins are saved to.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Site icons come from Chrome's own icon cache, so no outside service learns which sites are in your vault. The
        extension uses this information only to fill and save logins for you. It complies with the Chrome Web Store
        User Data Policy, including the Limited Use requirements.
      </p>

      <h2>The app and your vault</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>VisiPass's own vault</strong> is encrypted on your computer. Only you can unlock it; VisiOffice cannot
          read it or recover it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sync</strong>, if you turn it on, writes encrypted files to a folder you choose, such as one your
          Dropbox or iCloud already syncs. That provider stores the encrypted files under its own terms; it cannot read
          them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>1Password</strong>, if you use it as the backend, is reached through 1Password's own <code>op</code>
          program, under 1Password's privacy policy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The access log</strong> of what was requested and approved stays on your computer.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Coding agents</h2>
      <p>
        VisiPass is built so an agent uses a secret without seeing it: the agent gets references, and values go only
        to commands you approve, with output masked. VisiPass sends nothing to any AI provider. What your agent sends
        to its own provider is governed by that provider's policy.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        visipass.app is a static site on Cloudflare, which keeps standard request logs under its own privacy policy.
        Fonts load from Google Fonts. The site sets no cookies and runs no analytics. Its demos run entirely in your
        browser.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
      <p>
        VisiPass is open source, so you can check all of this in <a href={repo}>the code</a>. Changes to this policy
        are posted here and in the repository's history. Questions go to
        <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>GitHub issues</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy h2 {
    @apply mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white;
  }
  .privacy p {
    @apply mt-4 text-ink-300 leading-relaxed;
  }
  .privacy ul {
    @apply mt-4 space-y-3 list-disc pl-5 text-ink-300 leading-relaxed marker:text-ink-600;
  }
  .privacy strong {
    @apply text-ink-100 font-medium;
  }
  .privacy a {
    @apply text-tn-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-white;
  }
  .privacy code {
    @apply font-mono text-[0.9em] px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-ink-900 text-ink-200;
  }
</style>
